        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d29c5fca-5c0c-5af0-8a2f-cab857b2474a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Cases: Slaughter-House Cases, United States v. Darby Lumber Co., Ward v. Maryland, United States v. Harris\n- Concepts: Fourteenth Amendment, Privileges and Immunities Clause, Commerce Clause, freedom of movement, state citizenship, residency\n- Laws: Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938\n\nFacts:\n- Ward v. Maryland, 79 U.S. 418, was decided in 1871 and was later cited as part of a line of cases holding that authority under the Privileges and Immunities Clause was given to the states.\n- The Slaughter-House Cases, 83 U.S. 36, were decided in 1873 and became a pivotal early civil rights case.\n- The Slaughter-House Cases held that the Fourteenth Amendment protects the privileges or immunities of citizenship of the United States rather than the privileges and immunities of citizenship of a state.\n- United States v. Harris, 106 U.S. 629, was decided in 1883 and was later cited with Ward v. Maryland and the Slaughter-House Cases as consistent with the Court's view that this authority was given to the states.\n- United States v. Darby Lumber Co., 312 U.S. 100, was decided in 1941.\n- United States v. Darby Lumber Co. upheld the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938 and held that Congress could regulate employment conditions under the Commerce Clause.\n- Under United States law, state citizenship and residency are flexible, and no government approval is required to move between states except for people subject to certain court orders such as parole conditions or child-custody arrangements.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "ebb3f4bc-0998-58ff-9c22-22299b77f0af",
        "text": "Page: Slaughter-House Cases. Sentence: It was a pivotal case in early civil rights law and held that the Fourteenth Amendment protects the privileges or immunities of citizenship of the United States, not privileges and immunities of citizenship of a state.\nPage: United States v. Darby Lumber Co.. Sentence: United States v. Darby Lumber Co., 312 U.S. 100 (1941) , was a case in which the United States Supreme Court upheld the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938, holding that the U.S. Congress had the power under the Commerce Clause to regulate employment conditions.\nPage: Freedom of movement under United States law. Sentence: Under the \"privileges and immunities\" clause, this authority was given to the states, a position the Court held consistently through the years in cases such as \"Ward v. Maryland,\" 79 U.S. 418 (1871), the \"Slaughter-House Cases,\" 83 U.S. 36 (1873) and \"United States v. Harris,\" 106 U.S. 629 (1883).\nPage: U.S. state. Sentence: State citizenship and residency are flexible, and no government approval is required to move between states, except for persons covered by certain types of court orders (e.g., paroled convicts and children of divorced spouses who are sharing custody).",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
